<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ban Borić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1154">1154</a>–<a href="/years/1163">1163</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ban Borić was the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> known by name, recorded as ban in 1154 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. He fought on the Hungarian side against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and was drawn into the Hungarian succession disputes of the early 1160s, after which he disappears from the sources.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>How and when Borić came to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> is uncertain. The historian Vladimir Ćorović held that he was not a native Bosnian, and his origins are believed to lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, in the Grabarje area of the župa of Požega. Hungary had become the overlord of Bosnia in the 1130s, and historians disagree over whether Borić held Bosnia as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> appointed by the Hungarian king or stood beside him as an ally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He is first mentioned with certainty in 1154, during the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. The Byzantine historian John Kinnamos, who called him exarch of the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, records that he and a Bohemian detachment joined King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-ii-of-hungary">Géza II of Hungary</a> at the siege of Byzantine-held Braničevo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. When the Hungarians lifted the siege and moved west towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> sent a force against the Bosnian troops, but it ran into the main Hungarian army and was decisively defeated.</p><p>Borić is not mentioned again until 1163. Géza II had died on 31 May 1162, and his brothers Ladislaus and Stephen IV rose against the crowned heir, Stephen III. Borić supported Stephen IV, who took the throne with Byzantine help. In 1163 he witnessed a charter of Stephen IV at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a>, listed after the palatine Beloš, and with the king's permission he gave the village of Zdelje to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later in 1163 Stephen III defeated Stephen IV and pursued his supporters, among them Borić. The Gesta Hunnorum et Hungarorum, written late in the 13th century, says that a soldier named Gotfridus defeated the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> on the king's orders. After this Borić disappears from the record, and from about 1166 Bosnia was a Byzantine province. His descendants, sometimes called the Boričević, held lands on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> in the 13th century. An undated charter of his to the Benedictine monastery on Lokrum has been shown to be a 13th-century forgery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ban Borić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1154 <b>Ban Borić</b> is the first named ruler, a vassal of Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-bosnia">Banate of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.45,17.92&amp;year=1158" aria-label="Slavonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ban Borić"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4280 6165h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4280 6165h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.45,17.92&amp;year=1158">What was here in 1158? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
